{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-122504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy,$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=25?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $5$ centered at $(-1,0).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡t+25{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0a14cf659ca086305bb6642153d6d26a60304464bff17b388f1ec39aa3b4d80"} {"question" "q-122504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-5)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-5)\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0a14cf659ca086305bb6642153d6d26a60304464bff17b388f1ec39aa3b4d80"} {"question" "q-122263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={y}^{3},$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=36?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $6$ centered at $(1,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)j \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={y}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }((6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)^{3})\\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0a14cf659ca086305bb6642153d6d26a60304464bff17b388f1ec39aa3b4d80"} {"question" "q-122509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "36{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "36{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122540" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1-{y}^{2},$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122540" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at $(-3,0).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122540" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0a14cf659ca086305bb6642153d6d26a60304464bff17b388f1ec39aa3b4d80"} {"question" "q-122540" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122540" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122540" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=3-{y}^{2},$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3-(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =3-9{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3-9{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0a14cf659ca086305bb6642153d6d26a60304464bff17b388f1ec39aa3b4d80"} {"question" "q-122489" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122489" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122489" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ traversed from the point $(-5,0)$ to the point $(0,-5)$ in the third quadrant?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $5$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/2ec6f01ab00c17d4888dfefb37c2813139af9c412e99acd1a38680274b0f0ddd.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡t+25{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & ={e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0a14cf659ca086305bb6642153d6d26a60304464bff17b388f1ec39aa3b4d80"} {"question" "q-122578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "25{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "25{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "5{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "5{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "5{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\,{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36,$ traversed from the point $(6,0)$ to the point $(0,6)$ in the first quadrant?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $6$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/c1e3332017b795ed6733a0547e6e449fdba3874f1d1e755118a0687ccaf7448c.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\,{e}^{1-{y}^{2}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =36{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0a14cf659ca086305bb6642153d6d26a60304464bff17b388f1ec39aa3b4d80"} {"question" "q-122582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "36{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "36{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "36{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-6{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "6{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "36{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/761643a777650909b8c7ea5477b4fe246ac4032fe183df5d9ad5cb5e08ac1ecc.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ along a circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0a14cf659ca086305bb6642153d6d26a60304464bff17b388f1ec39aa3b4d80"} {"question" "q-122568" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122568" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122568" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\,\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,-3)$ to the point $(3,0)$ in the fourth quadrant?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/b7c2c6f9e50988629007a06213cb3acf0a48f9951f026fb70d5a986b760abdfe.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\,\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{9-(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{9-9{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }9{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0a14cf659ca086305bb6642153d6d26a60304464bff17b388f1ec39aa3b4d80"} {"question" "q-122588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "27{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "27{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "27{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "9{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "9{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "27{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/4403bd230338dee226429d3f8f3ac8eaede1152aff0b6b18bd2e7ad9f3a3b8e3.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=9-{x}^{2}$ along a circle of radius $3,$ traversed from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9-{x}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9-(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =9-9{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =9{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0a14cf659ca086305bb6642153d6d26a60304464bff17b388f1ec39aa3b4d80"} {"question" "q-122570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "27{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "27{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=9{sin}^{2}⁡t+1,$ $\\text{d}u=18\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{1}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0a14cf659ca086305bb6642153d6d26a60304464bff17b388f1ec39aa3b4d80"} {"question" "q-122585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =16{cos}^{2}⁡t-16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =16({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t,$ $\\text{d}u=2\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =32{\\int }_{0}^{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =32\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{4\\pi } \\\\  & =32(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0a14cf659ca086305bb6642153d6d26a60304464bff17b388f1ec39aa3b4d80"} {"question" "q-122136" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122136" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122136" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/e27774d260c053a7835b30e62c2d4b41be4b8dc22efcba8f7aa95704590561cf.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y-3}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as  \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y-3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3,$ $\\text{d}u=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3}\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{-7}^{-3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\,{e}^{u}{|}_{-7}^{-3} \\\\  & =4({e}^{-3}-{e}^{-7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{e}^{3}}-\\frac{4}{{e}^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{4}-4}{{e}^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0a14cf659ca086305bb6642153d6d26a60304464bff17b388f1ec39aa3b4d80"} {"question" "q-122441" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122441" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{1-{e}^{4}}{{e}^{7}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{e}^{7}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2+4{e}^{7}}{{e}^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{4{e}^{4}-4}{{e}^{7}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122441" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4{e}^{4}-4}{{e}^{7}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=y+1$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $5$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡t+25{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y+1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\,\\text{d}t & =5[-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =5[(-5+5)+(2\\pi -0)] \\\\  & =10\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0a14cf659ca086305bb6642153d6d26a60304464bff17b388f1ec39aa3b4d80"} {"question" "q-122580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "10\\pi "] ["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "5\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-122580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-40255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/cbd92b205693f1056e4dc2d0fc8154843e62b433b66d9b0ba8918d7f5bb3b8bf.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(-2,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-40255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{0}^{-2}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{-2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-40255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0a14cf659ca086305bb6642153d6d26a60304464bff17b388f1ec39aa3b4d80"} {"question" "q-40255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-40255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-\\frac{2}{e}"] ["math" "2-{e}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{e}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}-2"] ["math" "{e}^{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "q-40255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dbf16f50c20e1dc7bf1b88e3daba61f7a3149175b8a0f63a428cb8dc8791a0b"} {"question" "e-9438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = x^2-1,$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/example-9438.json" "file_sha256" "740ee19c8160b8c0847fb90847766c225985cccd28b23e7a250d76dd089a34cb"} {"question" "e-9438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-1 \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t-1)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t-1\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/example-9438.json" "file_sha256" "740ee19c8160b8c0847fb90847766c225985cccd28b23e7a250d76dd089a34cb"} {"question" "e-6305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = 2x+y^2$ along the quarter-circle $x^2+y^2=4,$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-2,0)$ in the second quadrant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/example-6305.json" "file_sha256" "5ecaf7f564734273bdbd88f4510cdf82864b26584506a2c02e9929b921c42649"} {"question" "e-6305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/d0ae53dd5d0932cbf834a166e851c227f75cb546b591a9fd43930ec06f0e9ddf.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = 2x+y^2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/example-6305.json" "file_sha256" "5ecaf7f564734273bdbd88f4510cdf82864b26584506a2c02e9929b921c42649"} {"question" "e-9462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y) =x^2-2$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/example-9462.json" "file_sha256" "3c5fe3e570fcd425b8175d2ed93589e52ac0e34c165b8b935d1474c4018668ec"} {"question" "e-9462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2-2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t-2 \\\\  & =2(2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t,$ $\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{0}^{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{4\\pi } \\\\  & =2(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/example-9462.json" "file_sha256" "3c5fe3e570fcd425b8175d2ed93589e52ac0e34c165b8b935d1474c4018668ec"}] "usage" {"q-122263" [] "e-9462" [] "e-6305" [] "q-122136" [] "e-9438" [] "q-40255" []} "basis" 1850 "retractions" [[17592186179890 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = x + y^{2}$, traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$?"] [17592186179888 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} - y^{2}$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^{2} + y^{2} = 16$."] [17592186180114 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3688/Source/Images/q-40255.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = xe^{y}$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$, traversed from the point $(-2, 0)$ to the point $(0,-2)$, as shown above."] [17592186179502 :question/problem "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = x^2-1$, traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1$."] [17592186179502 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$. It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-\\sin ti + \\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-\\sin t)^{2} + (\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2-1\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= (\\cos t)^{2} - 1 \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} t - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)ds &= ∫_{0}^{2π}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}(\\cos^{2} t - 1) \\cdot 1 \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}\\cos^{2} t - 1dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179456 :question/problem "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = 2x+y^2$ along the quarter-circle $x^2+y^2=4$, traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-2,0)$ in the second quadrant."] [17592186179456 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3688/Source/Images/ae378f2d7290f1c5b6d5c6b3f26dd450.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\pi\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-2\\sin ti + 2\\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-2\\sin t)^{2} + (2\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4(\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x+y^2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= 2(2\\cos t) + (2\\sin t)^{2} \\\\\n&= 4(\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)(2x + y^{2})ds &= ∫_{π/2}^{π}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/2}^{π}4(\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t) \\cdot 2 \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= 8∫_{π/2}^{π}(\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179509 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y) =x^2-2$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 4$."] [17592186179509 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$. It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 2\\pi\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-2\\sin ti + 2\\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-2\\sin t)^{2} + (2\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4\\sin^{2} t + 4\\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4(\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2-2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= (2\\cos t)^{2} - 2 \\\\\n&= 4\\cos^{2} t - 2 \\\\\n&= 2(2\\cos^{2} t - 1) \\\\\n&= 2\\cos 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)ds &= ∫_{0}^{2π}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}2\\cos 2t \\cdot 2 \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= 4∫_{0}^{2π}\\cos 2tdt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t$,\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4∫_{0}^{2π}\\cos 2tdt &= 2∫_{0}^{4π}\\cos udu \\\\\n&= 2\\sin u \\mid _{0}^{4π} \\\\\n&= 2(0 - 0) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186177329 17592186177330 17592186177331 17592186177332 17592186177333 17592186177341 17592186177342 17592186177343 17592186177344 17592186177345 17592186178560 17592186178561 17592186178562 17592186178563 17592186178564] "immutable_fields" [[17592186177328 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177328 :answer-field/id] [17592186177328 :answer-field/key] [17592186177328 :answer-field/type] [17592186177340 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177340 :answer-field/id] [17592186177340 :answer-field/key] [17592186177340 :answer-field/type] [17592186178559 :answer-field/correct] [17592186178559 :answer-field/id] [17592186178559 :answer-field/key] [17592186178559 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
